WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.359 --> 00:00:03.559
Sprechen sie Deutsch?
(Do you speak German?)

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.960
Deutsch? no, sorry, I don't speak Dutch

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.300
 

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.099
Hello language enthusiasts. Welcome to 
the Langfocus channel and my name is Paul

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:17.299
Today I'm going to talk about the German language.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.500
German is a very important language 
that deserves all of our attention.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:26.100
It is spoken by 95 million native speakers, 
mainly in central Europe

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:32.920
but it's also spoken by an additional 10 to 15 million 
second language speakers, especially in Eastern Europe.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.380
It is the majority language and 
an official language in Germany, of course

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:41.560
but also Austria, Switzerland, Liechtenstein
and the South Tyrol region of Northern Italy.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.220
It's also an official language in Luxembourg and Belgium 
but it's not a majority language there.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.940
German is the most widely spoken language 
in the European Union.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:58.000
It is also one of the most widely taught languages 
in the world with between 75 and 100 million people 
having studied it as a foreign language

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.840
In both, the United States and in Europe,
it is the third most widely taught foreign language.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.480
Did you know that one tenth of the world's books 
are published in German ?

00:01:07.599 --> 00:01:11.079
German is also the second most
widely used scientific language.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:17.460
I know some doctors and all of them have some reading 
knowledge of German because so much scientific 
research is documented in German.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.080
So are you starting to get an idea 
of why German is important?

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.280
 

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:29.880
German is a member of the West Germanic language family, 
which also includes Dutch, Afrikaans, Frisian, 
English and Yiddish

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.840
The West Germanic languages are part of 
the wider Germanic language family

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.200
So let's take a look at the origins 
of the Germanic language family.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.420
Wait, actually no. I did this in my last video. So...

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.360
I'm just going to play that part again 
while I go get a coffee and I'll be right back

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.960
All Germanic languages developed from Proto-Germanic 
which was spoken around 500 BCE

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:53.079
Proto-Germanic possibly originated in Scandinavia

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.000
and different varieties of Germanic
 began to emerge with migration.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:06.420
Runic inscriptions from the 2nd century CE show us that, 
by that time, Proto-Germanic had began to separate
into distinct Western, Eastern and Northern dialects.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.400
Oh sorry. Are you guys waiting for me?

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.240
 

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.240
Well, let's move on.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:20.120
The West Germanic dialects were probably closely 
related enough to be mutually intelligible 
until about the 8th century CE.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:27.439
But, at that time, something was happening that would 
split the Germanic language family and give birth to 
the German language as we know it today.

00:02:27.439 --> 00:02:31.919
That was the High Germanic Sound Shift 
(or the High Germanic Consonant Shift)

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.120
Some West Germanic varieties underwent 
a number of sound changes.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:42.280
Nine consonants changed to be precise and 
this created two distinct groups of Germanic dialects:

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:46.700
High German or Hochdeutsh 
and Low German or Plattdeutsh

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:49.959
And, just out of interest, let me pronounce 
these words a different way:

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.480
High Deutsch and Flat Deutsch.
Hmm... maybe there's a connection with English.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:59.700
High German dialects arose in the southern areas 
of Germany which are at a higher elevation.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.540
That's why they're called "High German".

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:07.200
The Low German dialects existed in the northern part of 
Germany at a lower elevation and also in the Netherlands.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.840
These terms do not refer to high and low status but 
rather to the elevation of the areas they were spoken in.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:18.200
High German can be further subdivided into 2 segments:
upper German and central German

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.780
So what were the sound changes that took place?
Well, let's take a look.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.800
These consonant changes took place in three stages:

00:03:25.159 --> 00:03:30.240
In the first stage, these consonants changed intervocalicly.
That means between vowel sounds.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.000
The p sound, the "puh" became a "fuh".

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:38.240
The "t" sound became a "ss"
and the "k" sound became a "ch"

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.879
Here's an example for the English word for "sleep".

00:03:40.879 --> 00:03:45.459
In Low German it's  "slapen",
but in German, it's "schlafen".

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:51.380
In the second stage, these consonants changed at the 
beginning of a word and also in some other context like

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:56.040
If they are the second letter in a double consonant 
or after an L or R

00:03:56.380 --> 00:04:00.479
So the sound "puh" became "pfuh" 
like a "p" and an "f" together.

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:06.039
the "t" sound became a "z"; it sounds like a TS sound,
even though it's written with a Z.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.500
And the K sound became a kch 
like a k sound followed by ch

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.260
here's an example for the english word "tame"

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.480
In Lower German, it's  "tamm",
 but in High German it's "zahm".

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.160
In the third stage, three more consonants changed :

00:04:20.439 --> 00:04:26.019
B sound became a P,
D sound became a T, and G sound became a K.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.680
Here's an example for the English word for "day":

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.020
In lower German it's  "dag",
but in High German, it's "tag".

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.560
All of these sound changes took place
in Upper German in the South.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:42.080
but some of them didn't take place in Central German
and some of them didn't take place in standard German.

00:04:42.199 --> 00:04:44.039
But we'll get to standard German in a minute.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.439
So some of you might be thinking :
"well, how did these sounds just switch?"

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:52.140
Well, they didn't instantly switch,
 they gradually changed over a period of a few centuries.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.420
probably between the 5th century and 8th century CE

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.920
Over the centuries, the dialect  continuum of Low German 
and High German grew more and more distinct

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:04.080
until there were varieties that 
were quite incomprehensible.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:09.200
So someone from the south in Bavaria would have trouble 
communicating with someone from Hamburg in the north.

00:05:09.399 --> 00:05:11.839
But, of course, that was before standard German.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:16.199
The dialects of Low German are often said 
to be a separate language from High German,

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:19.919
and they're often said to be halfway
 between High German and Dutch

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:26.360
Now the Low German dialect actually crosses 
the border into the eastern part of the Netherlands 
where Low German is also spoken.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:30.920
But these days the Low German dialects 
in the north of the country are mostly fading,

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:37.400
and basically only older people speak them. 
Younger people might understand them 
but they tend to use standard German instead

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.040
But when did standard German come into the picture?

00:05:40.319 --> 00:05:46.539
Standard German - which, by the way,  is also called 
Hochdeutsch in German - so don't be confused 
between High German and standard German -

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.240
originated as a written literary language 
that developed over a few hundred years.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:57.680
as writers tried to write in a way that would be 
understood by the largest number of people possible 
from different dialect areas.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:06.680
But perhaps the most important factor in the 
development of standard German was  the translation of 
the Bible into German by Martin Luther in the year 1522.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:13.700
He based his translation on the Saxon dialect of 
central German, while including some vocabulary 
from other dialects as well.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:21.280
Virtually all speakers of German dialects owned 
a copy of the new Bible and studied it effectively, 
popularizing this form of written German.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:25.600
Originally there was no single way to pronounce 
this written form of German but...

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.879
eventually the dialect of Hanover became the standard 
for pronunciation of standard German.

00:06:30.879 --> 00:06:35.739
 

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:39.960
Standard German includes some of the sounds 
of the High German Consonant Shift

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.740
but not all of them because it was influenced 
by the dialect of Hanover.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:48.740
So standard German began as a written language 
but as it became the language of education,

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:53.900
it became more widespread as the formal spoken language
and it also influence the local dialects.

00:06:54.279 --> 00:06:59.639
Today pretty much everyone can speak standard
German even if they speak a local dialect casually

00:06:59.639 --> 00:07:06.239
and many local dialects have become largely replaced by standard German, especially the Low German dialects in the north of the country.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:12.240
But there are still some dialects that are quite distinct 
and still thriving like the Bavarian dialect in the southeast.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:18.780
German is considered a pluricentric language. 
That means that there are multiple standard 
varieties  of the language

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:23.480
There is one standard language in Germany, 
one in Austria and one in Switzerland.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:28.080
All of these standard varieties are pretty much the same, 
except for some different vocabulary.

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:36.320
But, of course, those are the standard language. 
Switzerland and Austria and also South Tirol which is 
related to Austria have their own dialects as well

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:41.040
The Austrian dialects are related 
to the Bavarian dialects in South East Germany.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.560
And the Swiss dialects are related to
the alemannic dialects in southwest Germany.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:50.240
Maybe, you saw my video about the languages 
of Switzerland. It's this one right here.

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:57.000
And, in that video, I talked about how the Swiss 
German dialects are kind of incomprehensible
 to speakers of standard German,

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:00.920
unless they also speak an Alemannic dialect 
from the border area.

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.920
But, of course, that's talking about the Swiss German dialects.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:09.020
German-speaking Swiss can speak standard German, 
even if they tend to use it less than people in Germany.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:13.800
Of course, they might pronounce standard German 
with the different accent, but that's no big deal.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:16.680
In Austria and South Tirol, the situation is similar.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:23.780
There are dialects that might be hard to understand for 
people from some other German-speaking countries 
but, of course, standard German is the bridge.

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:32.240
These countries are all part of the German "Sprachraum" 
which means the geographic area over which 
that language is spoken.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:36.820
And within that  "Sprachraum", 
standard German is the "dachsprache"

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:41.240
That means the umbrella language under which 
all of those dialects can come together.

00:08:41.279 --> 00:08:46.279
Those 2 German terms I just used "Sprachraum" and "Dachprache" are common linguistic terms.

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:52.360
And I think that shows just how much influence 
the German language has over academic areas 
of research, like linguistics.

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:54.200
So, what is the german language like?

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:59.120
Well, it's closely related to English  which is obvious 
when we look at the most basic sentences.

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:00.120
Here's an example.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:08.480
"I buy books often" = "Ich kaufe häufig Bücher"

00:09:08.799 --> 00:09:15.279
Here, the word order is SVO, like an English except that
the adverb comes before the object instead of after it.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:17.759
Here's another example with an important twist.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:25.960
"I will buy the book today" = 
"Ich werde das Buch heute kaufen"

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:29.400
In this case, we see one of the most confusing things 
for learners of German,

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:32.679
which is the placement of the second verb 
at the end of the sentence.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:40.180
So if there's a helping verb like "will" or "werde", it is in 
the normal SVO position but the main verb goes at the end.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:41.399
Another example:

00:09:41.399 --> 00:09:49.179
[EN] "The book was interesting" =
[DE] "Das Buch war interessant"

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:50.640
And one more example :

00:09:50.879 --> 00:10:01.299
[EN "I like people who work hard"
[DE] "Ich mag Menschen, die hart arbeiten"

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.460
Another thing that learners of German find 
challenging is the case system in German.

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.080
In German, pronouns and nouns change form 
depending on their function in the sentence.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:14.600
but, along with them, the articles 
and adjectives also change.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:19.340
Here's an example of German grammatical cases, 
using the noun meaning TABLE (Rem: not "book")

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:35.180
You can see that the change takes place not only 
in the noun but also in the definite article as well.

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:38.399
The work for "table" is a masculine noun in German.

00:10:38.399 --> 00:10:43.379
But there are actually 3 genders in German. There are 
masculine nouns, feminine nouns and neuter nouns.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:51.200
And for each of them, the case forms are different and
all of these different forms also apply to adjectives, 
which drives learners crazy.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:55.540
So, while German is closely related to English, 
"the devil is in the details".

00:10:55.580 --> 00:11:03.920
Even though most of the other Germanic languages are 
classified as category 1 languages by the FSI,
German is classified as category 2.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:09.639
It's closely related to English and they have a lot in 
common in terms of the general syntax and vocabulary,

00:11:09.639 --> 00:11:13.779
but, when it comes to the fine details of grammar, 
there are some challenges for learners.

00:11:13.919 --> 00:11:17.379
But, as alwaysn if you are interested
in German-speaking cultures and countries,

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:22.840
or if you would like to benefit from the widespread use 
of German, then don't hesitate to start learning.

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:26.399
All challenges become adventures
when you become fascinated.

00:11:26.399 --> 00:11:28.139
The question of the day, German speakers:

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:33.159
Do you think there's anything unique or special 
or interesting about your local variety of German?

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:35.960
And how do other varieties of German sound to you?

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:39.960
Let us know in the comments down below. 
And everyone else, jump in and let's discuss.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:42.880
Thank you for watching and have a nice day.

